    "comment": "Analysis of the Temporal and Seasonal Distribution of Water Main Breaks\n\nThe analysis of the annual distribution of water main breaks in Calgary reveals a significant increase in the number of events between 1982 and 1986 compared with several other periods in the historical dataset.\n\nAn examination of the monthly distribution during these five years shows particularly high concentrations during the winter months, especially in 1982, 1984, and 1985. However, some variations are observed, such as in 1986, when October exhibits a notable peak.\n\nAcross the entire historical period, January, February, November, and December are among the months with the highest average number of water main breaks. The seasonal distribution also confirms that winter accounts for the highest number of events, followed by spring, autumn, and summer.\n\nThese observations suggest a possible association between cold weather conditions and the frequency of water main breaks.\n\nHowever, the presented charts do not yet establish a direct statistical relationship between temperature and water main breaks. This hypothesis must be investigated using historical meteorological data, particularly minimum temperatures, freezing events, and freeze-thaw cycles.\n\nFinally, the unusually high concentration of water main breaks between 1982 and 1986 warrants further investigation into the quality and comparability of historical records. This analysis will help distinguish actual variations in water main break occurrences from potential differences in historical data collection practices.",
